The SURCOUF study: Design and baseline characteristics
of a prospective population-based cohort on diabetes
care in France
L’étude SURCOUF. La prise en charge du diabète en France: étude
prospective en population générale. Plan de l’étude et caractéristiques
de base de la cohorte
In 2015, 3 million people were diabetic in France and the preva-
lence of the disease was estimated to be around 4.7% [1,2]. Contrary
to other countries, there is no large French cohort of patients with
diabetes [3]. Information concerning French diabetic patients are
scarce and incomplete, coming mainly from the French national
health insurance information system and retrospective studies
[4,5]. The objective of the SURCOUF study (SURvey on the COm-
plications and drUg treatments of diabetes in the western part of
France) was to establish a large cohort of diabetic patients, and
to describe their health status and therapeutic management. The
objective of this letter is to describe the method used to establish
this cohort and the baseline characteristics of the participants. The
SURCOUF cohort was initially designed as a cross-sectional study
aiming to recruit 10,000 diabetic patients between September 2012
and September 2015. It was secondarily transformed into a cohort
with follow-up visits planned every three years (CNIL 1526598).
The recruitment was performed from a network of general prac-
titioners (GP) and diabetologists from private ofﬁces  or hospital
departments located in the Western part of France. To be eligible
for the SURCOUF study, patients had to be over 18 years old and
diagnosed with diabetes for at least 1 year. After patient approval,
data were collected on a secure web-based database (IDBC Com-
pany, Saint-Herblain, France) in accordance with French safety
policies. The data were collected and recorded directly online by
the practitioners. A clinical research assistant regularly visited the
participating physicians to help them to recruit patients and to
ensure data quality. The collected variables are indicated in Table 1.
The data were anonymized for the overall analysis but not for the
physician in charge of the patients. The questionnaire could be com-
pleted in few minutes. The description of the clinical and biological
parameters was performed using R software Version 3.3.2.
