Approximately 320 physicians were contacted and 140 partic-
ipated including 95 general practitioners and 45 diabetologists.
The leading cause for not be involved in the study was the lack
of time. The goal of 10,000 patients was reached in December 2015
(39 months after the onset of the enrollment), and 9929 could be
analyzed. Patients were mostly recruited by diabetologists (88.5%)
and in hospitals (71.2%). The main clinical characteristics, metabolic
data and diabetic complications are presented in Table 1. Among
patients with type 1 diabetes (T1D), non-proliferative retinopa-
thy was the most common complication (14.3%). Among patients
with type 2 diabetes (T2D), the most common complication was
coronary heart disease (19.3%). These data report a contemporary
description of the clinical and treatment features of a large sam-
